From d2e5e666e711666fed945ce12e54499c93ab1457 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Garcia <44309076+ElGuslml@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:16:22 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 18/24] gpu: perform constant full-coverage draws as clears of
 the owning render targets

Patch 0017 stopped copying EDRAM into a render target that a draw
overwrites entirely, but when a game clears through an alias (another
MSAA mode and pitch than the scene's, with the same tile layout), the
alias still became the owner of the EDRAM and the scene's render
targets had to copy the cleared contents back on their next draw. In
Pirates of the Caribbean: At World's End that was the other half of the
cost: at a 2x2 resolution scale, 16.3 ms -> 14.8 ms per frame on an RTX
4070 Ti, and the GPU no longer saturates at 60 FPS (99 % -> 89 %).

New cvar render_target_draw_clears (default off). When set, and the draw
replaces every sample of a rectangle of all its bound render targets
(the output state checks of patch 0017), RenderTargetCache::Update first
tries TryDrawAsClear:

- The values written must be constant: the depth from the vertices
  (equal screen-space Z, no pixel shader depth, no polygon offset), the
  stencil from the reference, and the colors from running the pixel
  shader on the CPU with the shader interpreter, which is possible when
  it reads no textures, no position and only interpolators that the
  vertex shader (run for the coverage test) exported equal in every
  vertex. The colors are packed as the EDRAM holds them (8_8_8_8 with
  the PWL gamma, 2_10_10_10, 7e3 float, 16-bit and 32-bit floats; the
  fixed-point 16-bit formats depend on the backend's range handling and
  fall back).
- The covered rectangle is mapped through the tile layout (32-bit
  sample columns and rows) to rectangles of the host render targets that
  currently own that EDRAM: directly for an owner with the same pitch
  whose base is a whole number of tile rows away (any pixel rectangle),
  through whole tiles with Transfer::GetRangeRectangles otherwise. 32bpp
  and 64bpp owners get the value widened or narrowed.
- Each rectangle is cleared with the backend's resolve-clear code
  (PerformTransfersAndResolveClears with one render target and no
  transfers, which converts the guest-packed value to the host format),
  the ownership map is left as it is, and the draw is skipped.

Anything that cannot be proven falls back to the normal path. The
coverage test of patch 0017 now returns a rectangle instead of a height
(the primitive itself when it is an axis-aligned rectangle, otherwise
the widest band from the top of the scissor), so the cutout also covers
clears that do not start at the left edge or the top.

